pical storks (family Ciconiidae) with representatives in the Americas, east Africa, and southern and southeastern Asia. Two species have \"ibis\" in their scientific or old common names, but they are not related to these birds, and merely resemble some bald-headed ibises.","revisionId":1344416013,"title":"Mycteria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycteria","wikidataId":"Q1180273"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Budgies","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Budgerigar","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Budgerigars","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Melopsittacus","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Melopsittacus Gould, 1840","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2479797},"ottId":734817,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Melopsittacus","synonyms":["Melopsitta"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The budgerigar ( BUJ-ər-ih-gar, -⁠ə-ree-; Melopsittacus undulatus), also known as the common parakeet, shell parakeet or budgie ( BUJ-ee), is a small, long-tailed, seed-eating parrot native to Australia. Naturally, the species is green and yellow with black, scalloped markings on the nape, back, and wings.","revisionId":1372354796,"title":"Budgerigar","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budgerigar","wikidataId":"Q121221"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Cockandy","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Sea Parrot","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Bottle Nose","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Coulter Neb","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Tammie Norie","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Atlantic Puffin","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Fratercula arctica","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Fratercula arctica (Linnaeus, 1758)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2481353},"ottId":864865,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Fratercula arctica","synonyms":["Alca arctica","Fratercula arctica arctica","Fratercula arctica grabae","Fratercula arctica naumanni"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Atlantic puffin (Fratercula arctica), also known as the common puffin, is a species of seabird in the auk family. It is the only puffin native to the Atlantic Ocean; two related species, the tufted puffin and the horned puffin, are found in the northeastern Pacific.","revisionId":1375007520,"title":"Atlantic puffin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_puffin","wikidataId":"Q26685"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"African Grey Hornbill","source":"Integrated Taxonomic Information System (ITIS)"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Tockus nasutus","confidence":98,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Tockus nasutus (Linnaeus, 1766)","status":"SYNONYM","usageKey":2475959},"ottId":873953,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Tockus nasutus","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The African grey hornbill (Lophoceros nasutus) is a member of the hornbill family of mainly tropical near-passerine birds found in the Old World. It is a widespread resident breeder in much of sub-Saharan Africa and the southwest of the Arabian Peninsula.","revisionId":1314195664,"title":"African grey hornbill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_grey_hornbill","wikidataId":"Q27074817"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Blood Pheasant","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Ithaginis","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Ithaginis Wagler, 1832","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2474059},"ottId":979425,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Ithaginis","synonyms":["Itaginis","Ithagenes","Ithagenis","Ithaginus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The blood pheasant (Ithaginis cruentus) or blood partridge is a galliforme bird in the pheasant family Phasianidae and the only species in the genus Ithaginis. It is a relatively small, short-tailed pheasant that is widespread in the lower Himalayas ranging across North and East India, Nepal, Bhutan, South China and